# sample.tw: turning a row of logits into a token.
#
# Import it with:  import "std/sample" as sample
#
# `generate` in std/transformer takes the argmax, which is the right default for
# a test -- the same prompt gives the same continuation, so a generation can be
# asserted on -- and the wrong one for anything a person reads. Greedy decoding
# from a language model produces text that repeats, because the single most
# likely next token is very often the one that just occurred, and there is no
# mechanism in argmax that can ever break the loop.
#
# What is here is the standard set of fixes, as separate functions over a logits
# vector rather than as flags on one big `sample` call. Each takes [vocab]
# logits and returns [vocab] logits, so they compose by nesting, and any of them
# can be tested on its own against a vector written out by hand. `sample_token`
# at the bottom composes them in the conventional order for a caller who just
# wants a token.
#
# WHY THE RNG IS AN ARGUMENT
#
# These draw from `std/random`, whose generators are values, not a global
# stream. That is the whole point of sampling being reproducible: a caller holds
# an `Rng` from `new_rng(seed)`, threads it through the generation loop, and gets
# the same text every run, on every platform, regardless of what else in the
# program draws random numbers. The builtin `rand` would not give that -- it is
# one stream for the process, so adding a dropout layer somewhere else silently
# changes the story the model tells.
#
# Note that `std/random` is a `mode systems` module and this one is not. The
# import works and the generator threads through unchanged; only the `Rng` value
# crosses the boundary.

import "std/random" as rnd

# The value used to mask a rejected token, matching `nn.causal_mask`. It is a
# large negative number and not -inf for the same reason: exp(-1e9) is zero to
# every digit a float carries, while -inf would put 0 * -inf into any backward
# pass that touched this and make it NaN. Nothing here is differentiated today,
# but a masked logit that is merely very unlikely rather than structurally
# impossible is the safer object to hand back.
let REJECTED = -1000000000.0

# -- Temperature -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Divide the logits before the softmax. Because softmax is exponential, dividing
# by t < 1 spreads the probabilities apart (the model becomes more confident and
# more repetitive) and dividing by t > 1 pulls them together (more surprising,
# and past about 1.3 more incoherent). t = 1 is the distribution the model
# actually learned, and it is worth knowing that this is the only setting that
# is not a distortion.
#
# `temp` must be positive. At exactly zero this is a division by zero rather
# than the greedy limit it approaches, so the greedy case is handled by name in
# `sample_token` instead of by letting a zero fall through here.
fn temperature(logits, temp) = logits / temp

# -- Top-k -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Keep the k highest logits and reject the rest. The oldest of the truncation
# schemes and still a reasonable one: it puts a hard floor under how bad a token
# can be, which is what stops the rare catastrophic draw from the long tail that
# derails a whole continuation.
#
# Implemented as a threshold rather than as a scatter of the top k back into
# place, because Twill has no scatter. `topk` returns the k largest in
# descending order, so its last element is the k-th largest, and keeping
# everything at or above it keeps exactly the top k. Ties at the boundary keep
# more than k, which is what the mainstream implementations also do -- the
# alternative is breaking a tie between two equally likely tokens by their
# index, which is arbitrary in a way that pretending they are both in the set
# is not.
#
# k <= 0 or k >= vocab is a no-op, so a caller can pass 0 for "off" rather than
# branching around the call.
fn top_k_filter(logits, k) {
  let n = len(logits)
  if k <= 0 { logits }
  else if k >= n { logits }
  else {
    let kth = topk(logits, k, 0)[k - 1]
    where(greater_equal(logits, kth), logits, REJECTED)
  }
}

# -- Top-p, or nucleus -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Keep the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities sum to at least p, and
# reject the rest (Holtzman et al., 2020).
#
# The argument against top-k that this answers: a fixed k is wrong at both ends
# of the same generation. After "the capital of France is" the model is nearly
# certain and k = 50 admits 49 tokens that are all wrong; mid-sentence in open
# prose a thousand tokens are genuinely plausible and k = 50 cuts off most of a
# legitimate distribution. Nucleus sampling makes the size of the candidate set
# follow the confidence the model itself reports, which is the quantity that was
# varying all along.
#
# Sort the probabilities descending, scan the cumulative sum, and find the first
# position where it reaches p. `sum(less(c, p))` counts the positions strictly
# below p, which is exactly the index of that first position, and it does the
# search without a loop. That index is clamped to the last element so a p at or
# above 1 keeps everything rather than running off the end.
#
# Then threshold on the probability at that position, the same trick top-k uses,
# so tokens tied with the boundary token are kept. At least one token always
# survives: when the very first cumulative entry already reaches p the count is
# zero, the threshold is the largest probability, and the argmax is kept.
#
# p >= 1 is a no-op. p <= 0 leaves only the most likely token, which is greedy.
fn top_p_filter(logits, p) {
  let n = len(logits)
  if p >= 1.0 { logits }
  else {
    let probs = softmax(logits, 0)
    # The nucleus is almost always a short prefix of the sorted probabilities, so
    # take a bounded top set (topk is a partial selection, not a full sort) and
    # read the cutoff from it. Fall back to sorting the whole vocabulary only when
    # that set does not yet hold p of the mass, which needs a very flat
    # distribution. In the fast path the threshold is identical to the full sort:
    # every probability past the prefix is already inside the nucleus and so adds
    # nothing to the count of entries below p.
    let cap = if n < 2048 { n } else { 2048 }
    let top = topk(probs, cap, 0)
    let ctop = cumsum(top, 0)
    if cap >= n or item(ctop[cap - 1]) >= p {
      let below = int(item(sum(less(ctop, p), 0)))
      let cut = if below >= cap { cap - 1 } else { below }
      let thresh = item(top[cut])
      where(greater_equal(probs, thresh), logits, REJECTED)
    } else {
      let ordered = sort(probs, 0, 1)
      let cum = cumsum(ordered, 0)
      let below = int(item(sum(less(cum, p), 0)))
      let cut = if below >= n { n - 1 } else { below }
      let thresh = item(ordered[cut])
      where(greater_equal(probs, thresh), logits, REJECTED)
    }
  }
}

# -- Drawing -----------------------------------------------------------------

# A categorical draw from a [vocab] probability vector, returning the index.
#
# Inverse-CDF: take a uniform u in [0, 1), and return the first index whose
# cumulative probability exceeds it. `sum(less(cum, u))` counts the entries
# whose running total is still at or below u, which is that index, so the search
# is one scan and one reduction rather than a loop over the vocabulary.
#
# The clamp on the result is not decoration. The cumulative sum of a softmax
# ends at 1 only up to rounding, and when it ends at 0.99999999 a u above that
# counts every entry and gives an index one past the end. It happens about once
# in a hundred million draws, which is often enough to matter in a long
# generation and rare enough to be unreproducible if it were left to crash.
fn categorical(r, probs) {
  let n = len(probs)
  let u = rnd.uniform(r)
  let idx = int(item(sum(less(cumsum(probs, 0), u), 0)))
  if idx >= n { n - 1 } else { idx }
}

# A categorical draw straight from logits.
#
# The softmax here is the builtin, which subtracts the row maximum before
# exponentiating. That matters more for sampling than anywhere else in the
# library, because a filtered logits vector deliberately contains -1e9 entries
# next to ordinary ones: `exp` of the raw values would be `exp(-1e9)` = 0
# alongside `exp(20)`, and a model whose logits have drifted to a few hundred
# would give `inf / inf` = NaN and then an index of zero, which reads as the
# model having strong opinions about token 0.
fn sample_logits(r, logits) = categorical(r, softmax(logits, 0))

# The greedy choice, for completeness and so `sample_token` has something to
# return at temperature zero.
fn greedy(logits) = int(item(argmax(logits, 0)))

# -- The composition ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Temperature, then top-k, then top-p, then draw. That order is the one every
# mainstream implementation uses and it is not arbitrary: temperature has to
# come first because it changes the probabilities that top-p is measuring, and
# applying it afterwards would mean the nucleus was chosen from a distribution
# the model is not actually sampling from. Top-k before top-p is a cheap
# pre-truncation of the tail that top-p would mostly discard anyway.
#
# The disabling values are the identity ones, so a caller names only what they
# want: `sample_token(r, row, 1.0, 0, 1.0)` is an honest draw from the model,
# `sample_token(r, row, 0.0, 0, 1.0)` is greedy, and
# `sample_token(r, row, 0.8, 40, 0.95)` is roughly what a chat model ships with.
fn sample_token(r, logits, temp, k, p) {
  if temp <= 0.0 {
    greedy(logits)
  } else {
    sample_logits(r, top_p_filter(top_k_filter(temperature(logits, temp), k), p))
  }
}
